// The boundary of the rule beside it: an **empty** container literal at a generic call determines
// nothing, so it pins nothing.
//
// A non-empty literal reports what it holds, and that is what instantiates the callee's type
// parameter. `[]` holds nothing to read an element type off, so the parameter stays open — and a
// callee that consumes the instantiation through a hidden slot (here `type_name::<T>()`, a runtime
// type name) has no answer to consume. That is E0023 at CHECK time with the spelling that fixes it,
// not a `"dyn"` invented at run time.
//
// Pinned as its own case because the fix for the non-empty literal must not reach this far: an
// expectation with nothing to say is not an expectation, but an argument with nothing to say is not
// an instantiation either, and inventing one from the callee's own parameter is the defect this
// pair fences off from both sides.
// expect: error E0023 at 21:6
// expect: exit 1

fn of_list<T>(xs: List<T>): string {
    return type_name::<T>();
}

echo of_list([]);
